   "reminder": "Earlier instructions still in force:\n- It's a built-in module that will enable you to set breakpoints, watch variables, and interact with your code during execution.\n- Yes, there are a few things to keep in mind.\n- First, learn how to set breakpoints in your code.\n- This allows you to pause the execution at specific lines and inspect the state of your program.\n- Second, understand how to step through your code, line by line, to observe the changes in variable values and program flow.\n- Finally, take advantage of conditional breakpoints, which allow you to specify conditions under which the program should pause, making it easier to isolate specific scenarios.\n- One challenge can be understanding the flow of control, especially when dealing with nested functions or complex control structures.\n- Make sure you take the time to learn how to navigate through stack frames and understand the call hierarchy.\n- Additionally, keep in mind that pdb may not always provide the most user-friendly experience, but it's a powerful tool once you're comfortable with it.\n- If you encounter situations where pdb feels limiting, you can explore other debugging tools or integrated development environments (IDEs) that offer more advanced debugging features while still utilizing pdb under the hood.",
